section_reviews={
'FOUNDATIONS-SPIKES':('retain','Rates describe expected counts rather than regular spike spacing. Synapses, feedback and tuning are taught before the population vector; neuron identity is separated from physical position. The local linear tuning rule has rate/slope units and a negative-rate restriction. Spatial maps are separated from finite measured maps; CA3/CA1 claims match the saved 2026 source introduction. No later memory calculation relies on unprovided anatomy.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-POISSON':('retain','The count law, mean, variance and optional factorial-moment proof agree. One-cell silence probability and pooled relative error recompute. The apparent independence claim is explicitly conditional on modeled state before discussing recurrent correlations. CV and Fano factor are separately defined and not equated. The figure caption correctly describes discrete estimates and the common per-cell scale.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-COVARIANCE':('retain','Centered-product covariance, variance of sums, nonzero-variance correlation and the MSD split are algebraically correct. Zero covariance is not mistaken for independence. The later eye inference is explicitly deferred to its signal/gain model. These identities supply the later readout cross-time covariance argument.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-ATTRACTOR':('retain','Fixed states, restoring directions and neutral direction are distinguished. Adding and subtracting the two rate equations yields the claimed x decay and invariant angle. Both numerical perturbations recompute. The example is explicitly a construction, and the later nonperpendicular projection is not claimed to be supplied by this geometry.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-DIFFUSION':('retain','Independence and zero-mean increment assumptions precede variance addition. Brownian increments are defined beside their first equation and before their later network use. RMS units, equal-coordinate multidimensional condition, bias-versus-spread arithmetic and min(s,t) covariance all check. Ring/boundary and variable-D limits are visible. The optional Poisson derivation is not needed to interpret the subsequent variance law.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-RANDOMNESS':('revise figure labels only','The text accurately separates a fixed expected-rate map from time-varying internal position. It allows both kinds of variation without claiming one measures the other. The map caption and alt preserve this distinction. Actual SVG/PNG chapter labels are stale, recorded in M-S1-02.'),
'MEMORY-SPIKES':('retain','A unit jump plus exponential decay gives the stated activation sum after initial transients. W indexing and transfer function roles are defined. The compensated count moments and Brownian covariance rewrite match main7–11 and SI1–6 to leading order. The many-cell explanation keeps mean fixed and input variance proportional to 1/n. Rates need not be individually Gaussian. Attractor tuning is kept as an assumption.'),
'MEMORY-LINEARIZATION':('retain','Taylor terms give K=-I/tau+diag(phi prime)W. Differentiating fixed points gives Ku=0. Normalization vTu=1 and vTK=0 remove decaying modes and retain coordinate changes. The tilted example integrates to x infinity=x0+(a/lambda)y0, explaining why perpendicular projection can fail. The figure chosen a/lambda=1/2 and initial y=2 give a shift of one. Linear algebra belongs to the audience baseline; specialist left/right roles are taught.'),
'MEMORY-DIFFUSION':('retain','Projecting independent Brownian increments yields 2D=sum(v squared r). Locality, weak-noise order and coefficient-variation timescale limits are stated. The tensor covariance formula retains possible coordinate coupling even with independent spike-generation noise. Systematic mean drift is not inferred from variance alone.'),
'MEMORY-SYMMETRIC':('retain','Transpose calculation gives v proportional to g prime only with symmetric W. The normalization supplies tau and the squared denominator. The numerator square is required by the earlier projection and SI18; source main2 visibly omits it. Mutual inhibition has neutral difference and stable sum, valid only at positive rates. b=20,tau=.1 gives rate sum200 and D100, as caption/alt claim.'),
'MEMORY-LONG-TIME':('retain','The zero-drift Ito convention is stated before the density equation. Gaussian normalization, wrap sum, uniform angular limit and pi squared/3 error check. The optional test-function derivation supplies the PDE; main text gives the moment derivation needed for variable-D expansion. The t squared coefficient is D D double-prime. Mutual excitation projection gives D=theta/(4tau), reconciling SI41 rather than silently following SI40. Nearby valid-domain qualification is retained; no claim is made to continue a negative-rate process across a boundary.'),
'MEMORY-DRIFT-SCOPE':('retain','Mean velocity is separated from random spreading. Ito/Stratonovich comparison gives D prime/2 drift because b b prime = D prime. Scaling dt/N versus square-root(dt/N) is correct. Source expressly says exact drift needs microscopic analysis. The Q covariance extension is immediate projection algebra and the likelihood qualification prevents using Poisson information unchanged for correlated models.'),
'MEMORY-FIGURES':('retain','Original source pages2–3 were visually inspected. Fig1 flow projection and storage/readout errors are correctly separated. Fig2 rate traces versus spike marks, dashed sigmoidal bound, angular wrap flattening and mutual inhibition example agree with panels and captions. Simulation checks are not presented as animal data.'),
'MEMORY-FI':('retain','Likelihood, score and squared-score information are taught in order. Conditional Poisson variance eliminates cross terms and supplies J=sum(r prime squared/r). Positive-rate and regularity limits are present. The Cramer Rao derivation correctly uses local unbiasedness and zero mean score; efficiency is limited to the informative weak-noise Gaussian regime. Baseline curve raises noise while keeping its derivative fixed, so information falls on nonzero-slope flanks.'),
'MEMORY-BOUND':('retain','Cauchy Schwarz with a=v sqrt(r), b=r prime/sqrt(r) and vTu=1 proves 2DJ>=tau inverse squared without symmetric W. Pointwise equality condition and c normalization are correct. Exponential transfer is sufficient; the b0=0 linear-family counterexample satisfies equality at every positive theta and validly limits the source converse. Mutual-inhibition numbers and strict inequality recompute. Matrix residual Q has correct dimensions and gives the positive-semidefinite difference, with invertible information explicitly required.'),
'MEMORY-COMPARISONS':('revise one first-use gap','Controlled N, firing scale and tau comparisons keep their conditions, and the two different tau exponents concern different held quantities. Integral change of variables gives J entries proportional to w^(d-2); fixed total firing instead gives w^-2. The word isotropic and its role in a scalar per-coordinate inversion are not taught before the required use at line232; see M-S1-01. Sensory/internal information equality remains conditional on matching tuning.'),
'MEMORY-READOUT-SETUP':('retain','Storage and future observation intervals and the desired cue state are distinct. Covariance vanishes under local unbiasedness conditioned on storage history. Translation invariance makes the conditional variance common across cue values. Boundaries and priors are excluded. Gaussian equivalent observations explicitly introduce independent process and observation errors as a local approximation to SI58–63, not a finite-bin exact Poisson assertion.'),
'MEMORY-NAIVE':('retain','Earlier Brownian covariance is recalled before its double integral. The two triangle integral is L cubed/3. Independent count error adds 1/(JL), giving 2DL/3+1/(JL). Differentiating gives sqrt(3/(2DJ)) and minimum (2/sqrt3)sqrt(2D/J). More observations harm this specific equal-weight estimator at long L.'),
'MEMORY-IDEAL':('retain','Gaussian precision and addition are taught through multiplying likelihoods. Backward transition adds 2Dh before earliest precision Jh is added. The small-h limit gives P prime=J-2DP squared. Defined tanh/artanh solve it and give the coth variance, agreeing with SI75 despite the S71 printed sign. Short expansion and long saturation ratios recompute. Characteristic saturation time is not an abrupt end to benefit; variance versus standard deviation factors are correctly separated.'),
'MEMORY-PARTIAL':('retain','Random independent thinning gives alpha J because rates and derivatives both scale by alpha. Diffusion of the full network stays fixed. The observation times and asymptotic variance scale as alpha^-1/2. A biased subset need not carry that information fraction. Full/ten-percent numerical examples and additive two-second storage errors recompute.'),
'MEMORY-EXTENSIONS':('retain','Rate relaxation and noise placement are stated before the block matrix. Its lower-right block must be negative from the preceding differential equation. Left-vector block equations and tangent normalization give tau+tau_m. The restriction to this two-stage model prevents a general additive-time claim. Constant additive Gaussian strength q gives matched information and projection factors, and its different equality condition is explained by the noise weighting.'),
'MEMORY-FIG3-AND-CAUTION':('retain','Source Fig3 was visually inspected; normalized ideal coth(z), naive z/3+1/z and dotted optimum agree. Readout curves omit storage variance. The inequality algebra shows sqrt(2D/J)<=2D tau, so using it as a lower bound is invalid. The strict numerical case is internally consistent with the local Gaussian model and explicitly not an author correction. Diffusion reduces integral accuracy without introducing a restoring leak; the closing empirical conditions retain the modeling limits.')
}

